{
  "result": "The first thing Mara noticed about the town was that nobody hurried when it rained. They stepped out of shops, opened their umbrellas, and continued whatever conversation the weather had interrupted. A butcher once stood ankle-deep in a gutter, explaining to a customer why he no longer believed in ghosts.\n\nMara had come to empty her father\u2019s house. She had allotted three days: one for papers, one for furniture, and one for the kitchen, where he kept screws in teacups and tea in a tin labeled SCREWS. On the first morning, she found a pear still ripening on the windowsill.\n\nShe could remember buying it. Her father had wanted one pear, not two, and she\u2019d teased him for making her stand in line for a single piece of fruit. He\u2019d said he liked having something to look forward to on Thursday. At the time she\u2019d thought he was being difficult.\n\nOutside, rain darkened the brick wall. The neighbor was carrying two chairs from his shed to his porch. On his second trip, he waved at Mara through the window and pointed to the empty chair. She held up the pear. He went inside and came back with a kitchen knife.\n\nThey ate it in quarters, leaning forward so the juice wouldn\u2019t run down their sleeves. The neighbor told her that her father had borrowed the chairs last summer and returned them painted blue. She asked what color they\u2019d been before. He looked down, as if the wood might remind him.",
